\par }\pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 As the world becomes smaller and more greatly interconnected, the importance of a well secured web site grows.  The Internet has brought people together much as the airplane and telephone did years ago by lowering 
the cost of travel and communication.  No longer are people hours or minutes apart, but mere microseconds.  To borrow a phrase, we can truly ``reach out and touch someone.''  Due to the nature of the Internet, though, we do not always know who is trying 
to touch us.}
\par {\fs20 Because of our innate desire to know who is on the other side of the door when we answer a knock, a process for identifying people has been developed.  This process is called authentication, and the decision to accept or deny a request is 
made purely on the ability of the person to identify himself.}
\par {\fs20 While much discussion concerning security can be made quite general, we will be looking specifically at the environment provided by the hypertext transfer protocol (HTTP).  Most transactions can be made quite secure by using Transport Layer 
Security (TLS), but in the interest of providing useful information, we will consider the nontrivial solutions in which the communication is open and available to third-party observers.}
\par {\fs20 After a brief overview of some basic methods that can be used when investigating protocols and methods and the potential risks associated with them, we will look at how those methods can be used in the environment that is present at Texas 
A&M University (TAMU) and why a secure authentication scheme is important there.  We will then cover general security concerns present in most protocols using authentication, followed by specific methods available within the HTTP standard.  Finally, 
we will examine a method developed at TAMU which addresses most of the concerns raised in the earlier discussions.}
\par {\fs20 In the rest of this paper, a browser is any client under the HTTP standard which can connect to a web site, which is any HTTP server.  The bit of information that a user can give to a web site to prove their identity is the secret (e.g., 
password).  A person is identified by a particular secret in the context of a username or other publicly available information, since it is only the secret that distinguishes the true person from an imposter in that context.  The collection of secrets 
and other information used to confirm an identity is held in an identity repository.  A remote web site is any web site not under the direct control of the person or group controlling the authentication database.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Methodology}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 The first task in either adopting an existing protocol or designing a new one is to identify the constraints placed upon the protocol by the environment in which it is to operate.  Only after all these constraints 
are identified should a solution be found that can fit the constraints.  Never beat the constraints to match a method, and never beat a method to match the constraints.  If the good fit is cannot be found, then designing a new method should be considered..}
\par {\fs20 When evaluating protocols, the complete environment in which the protocol is to exist must be considered.  This includes the network, both intranet and extranet (everything not in the intranet), the clients, and the servers.  These three 
areas are quite large in scope.  The clients, for example, include not only the hardware and the operating system running on that hardware, but also the system administrators and any users.}
\par {\fs20 When comparing protocols, some measure of security must be made for the comparisons to be objective.  We will develop in later sections some mathematical tools to help with this part of the evaluation.  We will consider such factors as the 
length of the request in time, the potential number of sniffers, and the lifetime of the sensitive information (e.g., secrets used for identification).  The result will be some measure of the risk of a security compromise for the particular methods or 
protocols being studied.}
\par {\fs20 Another important factor in choosing a protocol is the effort required on the part of other system administrators and end users in the implementation.  The most desirable protocol requires zero effort on the part of others which might not 
be as intimately familiar with the details.  The less effort required, the more likely others will readily accept the particular proposed method.  A method with a manageable security risk can be more secure at times than a method with no risk and no 
implementation.}
\par {\fs20 If after evaluating the existing protocols, no protocol is found that will meet the needs of the organization, a new protocol may be developed.  This new protocol should meet the requirements demanded of the other methods as much as possible. 
 One of the best ways to evaluate any new protocol is to write an `RFC'-like document and use this document to identify weak areas in the protocol.  An example is provided at the end of this paper for the }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  methods.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 TAMU Environment}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 World wide, the Apache web server is used on over half the web sites while Microsoft server products account for approximately a quarter of the sites [14].  At TAMU, this is reversed, with Microsoft products 
serving slightly more than half the web sites while Apache serves approximately a third of the sites.}
\par {\fs20 The TAMU network is actually a small picture of the Internet.  The networking group within Computing and Information Services (CIS), the central department of TAMU computer services, manages the campus network, while the departments are allowed 
to manage their own networks within their buildings.  The departments are able to contract with the networking group for network installation, but this is not a requirement.  As a result, different departments can have different network characteristics.}
\par {\fs20 The interdepartmental, or campus network implements anti-sniffing measures while some department networks do not, especially those not installed by the CIS network group.}
\par {\fs20 As a result of the TAMU network environment, most systems can be considered to live on a fairly secure network, and those portions of the network that are considered insecure are fairly well known.  However, as soon as the network connection 
leaves the TAMU network, no security can be assumed.}
\par {\fs20 In the TAMU network environment, then, any connection coming from within the TAMU network can be considered sniff-proof with a high degree of reliability.  Any connection coming from outside the TAMU network should be considered sniffed at 
all times.}
\par {\fs20 With a distributed environment like that at TAMU, not only must the network be considered, but also the system administrators and webmasters of remote websites.  Many web applications at TAMU rely on the central directory (currently PH) for 
authentication information.  However, not everyone is allowed access to all the information contained within the directory.  Allowing remote system administrators access to a person's PH authentication credentials grants them access to all the information 
in the directory accessible with those credentials and also allows them access to other web applications.}
\par {\fs20 In addition to the webmasters and system administrators being themselves an unknown risk, the security of the actual machines is also unknown.  If the web applications are poorly written and the machine is insecure, an attacker could gain 
access to the machine and be able to monitor the memory to obtain sensitive information, including the authentication information.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Security Concerns}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 With any authentication scheme, several security issues must be considered.  These range from storing secrets to actual interaction with the browser to securing against third parties.}
\par {\fs20 Mark Twain was to have said once that ``three people can keep a secret if two of them are dead.''  This is especially true in the computer world.  For a secret to truly work, only the person whom the secret identifies should know it.  The 
secret should never be known by the server or any other process.  One-way maps, or hashes, are best for this purpose.  The server is able to store the hash of the secret, which is then known only by the person.  When the person authenticates, the server 
can take the provided secret, hash it through the same process as before, and compare with the stored results.  If they match, then the person knows the correct secret.  Given a sufficiently strong hash, no one else, not even the server, can know the 
secret.  As soon as two entities (people, processes, etc.) know the proper password in a persistent manner, the identity the secret represents and protects is open to impersonation.}
\par {\fs20 Of course, if the browser must authenticate to a web site, knowledge of the secret must be transmitted in some form from the one to the other.  The simplest protocols require open transmission of the secret.  This allows third parties to 
listen in and capture the secret along with any other identifying information.  While the client and server could use TLS to encrypt the session and thereby disallow third party inspection of any transactions, doing so places a heavy computational burden 
on both the browser and the web site.}
\par {\fs20 When the identity repository is maintained on the same machine as the web site requiring authentication, the only security concerns are protecting the secret in transit and in the repository.  However, when the secrets are stored in a central 
repository that is then used by remote web sites, additional security risks must be considered.}
\par {\fs20 Keeping the previous proscription in mind against multiple entities having knowledge of the secret, consider the case of remote web sites querying a central database to confirm the authority of a given secret, as at TAMU.  Each request for 
a protected resource results in network traffic between the remote site and the central identity repository.  With basic authentication, the remote site will know the secret but can conceal it in it's transactions with the identity repository.  With 
digest authentication, the secret must be available in the identity repository and passed to the remote site.  In either situation, the remote site gains access to the person's identity.  In certain situations, this access is unacceptable, and lowering 
that risk is worth slightly higher risk in other areas.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Measuring Risk}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Using some fairly simple mathematics, we can form a crude measure of the risk inherent in a particular protocol. After exploring these crude measures, we will look at a little of the more advance probability 
theory available and how it might apply to measuring the inherent risk in a protocol.}
\par {\fs20  We will begin by defining a few functions and variables.  Each transaction has a window of opportunity during which anyone sniffing the network has access to potentially sensitive information.  We will denote this window by the variable 
}{\i\f3\fs20 t}{\fs20 .  }
\par {\fs20 The number of sniffers on a network with access to and an interest in a particular bit of information is a function of time since once a sniffer has the information, further sniffing of the secret does not affect the probability it will be 
used.  We will therefore denote the number of sniffers as }{\i\fs20 s}{\fs20 (}{\i\fs20 t}{\fs20 )}{\fs20 .  The probability that a particular sniffer can catch part of the transaction in the window and be able to make use of it is }{\f3\fs20 rt}{
