\par \pard\plain \qj \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Differentiating both sides of the equation leads to the following definition for }{\i\fs20 s}{\fs20 (}{\i\fs20 t}{\fs20 ):}
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb180\sa108\tqc\tx2429\tqr\tx4680 \f1\fs20\cf1 \tab
\par \pard\plain \qj \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Thus, the percentage of sniffers which have successfully sniffed the transaction is}
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\fi629\sb180\sa108\tqc\tx2429\tqr\tx4680 \f1\fs20\cf1 \tab
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 If the network is considered fairly secure, then }{\f3\fs20 r}{\fs20  will be close to zero, but if the network is insecure (e.g., connections from the Internet), then }{\f3\fs20 r}{\fs20  will be much greater 
than zero.  For the truly paranoid, }{\f3\fs20 r}{\fs20  can be considered one unless the network and protocol can be proven secure against sniffers.  }{\i\fs20 T}{\fs20  should be considered the number of transactions, which is inherently discrete. 
Since }{\i\fs20 T}{\fs20  is discrete, }{\fs20 the previously developed equations can only be considered approximations to be used for comparisons between methods.}
\par {\fs20 Seeing how quantities might evolve in time can be nice but not always helpful.  Doing some manipulation, we can find the number of transactions}{\fs20  which will result in half the sniffers sniffing the secret:}
