\par \pard\plain \qj \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Since we do not know the value of }{\i\fs20 s}{\dn6\fs16 0}{\fs20 , we can combine it with }{\i\fs20 t}{\fs20  to produce a new quantity }{\i\fs20 T}{\dn6\fs16 1/2}{\dn6\fs20  which we can use to compare authentication 
methods:}
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb180\sa108\tqc\tx2429\tqr\tx4680 \f1\fs20\cf1 \tab
\par \pard\plain \qj \f1\cf1 {\fs20 The larger }{\i\fs20 T}{\dn6\fs16 1/2}{\fs20 , the less risky the authentication method.}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 For a more rigorous approach, consider the methods developed in [6,7,11].  These references develop some probability theory and present some statistical methods in physics which we will now use to explore some 
of the complexities in modelling authentication mechanisms and their information flow.  Some authentication methods (e.g., basic authentication) can be modelled as a Markov chain.  These are much simpler to model than others which cannot be so modelled 
(e.g., digest authentication).}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 HTTP Authentication}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 The hypertext transfer protocol (HTTP) version 1.1 provides for two authentication methods [4].  The older, and less secure, of the two is basic authentication.  The newer one is digest authentication.  Both 
work within the protocol and require special knowledge within the browser.}
\par {\fs20 Basic authentication can not be considered secure unless used in conjunction with encrypted channels such as TLS.  With every authenticated request, the username and password are sent as plaintext.  However, because the password is readily 
available to the web server, the usernames and passwords may be stored in any format on the server.  This allows for effective protection of the passwords in the case of a security breach on the server.  Almost all browsers support basic authentication. 
 With basic authentication, we can consider }{\f3\fs20 r}{\fs20  to be close to unity while }{\f3\fs20 t}{\fs20  is close to 0.028 seconds.  These lead to a value for }{\i\fs20 T}{\dn6\fs16 1/2}{\fs20  of about 40.}
\par {\fs20 Digest authentication is designed to do only one thing better than basic authentication.  No passwords pass over the network in a usable fashion.  The username, realm, and password are hashed together to form an ``effective'' password which 
is then combined with other information (e.g., a nonce and the URL) and passed back to the web server with the request.  The password is never passed from the browser to the server, and the server must store the hash of the username, realm, and password. 
 Because this information must be available to the web server, security of the machine is more important than in the case of basic authentication since any attack yielding the password database trivially opens the site to attack via HTTP.  Few browsers 
support digest authentication [5,8,13].  With digest authentication, we should consider }{\f3\fs20 r}{\fs20  to be near zero, yet positive, and }{\f3\fs20 t}{\fs20  to be approximately twice that for basic authentication.  These lead to a value for }{
\i\fs20 T}{\dn6\fs16 1/2}{\fs20  of more than 2}{\up6\fs16 32}{\fs20 .}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Authentication with Cookies}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Because basic authentication is insecure with the authentication information passing across the network with each request and digest authentication does not enjoy widespread support among browsers, several authentication 
schemes have been developed using cookies.  These schemes allow the user's credentials to pass over the network once, either in the clear (usually) or obfuscated (e.g., a Java applet).  After the user has authenticated, a cookie is set containing a session-id 
which can then be used to track the user as they browse through the web site.  These schemes thus combine the wide support of cookies with the relative security of digest authentication.}{  }{\fs20 However, like basic and digest authentication, the web 
site must have intimate knowledge of the user.  In this case, the session-id is used to track user information for the current session and is devoid of any intrinsic value.}
\par {\fs20 Let us take a look at  a few of the Perl modules available on the Comprehensive Perl Archive Network (CPAN) for managing cookies with session-ids.  }{\f2\fs20 Apache::AuthCookie}{\fs20  provides authentication and authorization using cookies. 
 }{\f2\fs20 Apache::Session}{\fs20  maintains persistent information on a per-user basis across connections.  Either package can be used to build a session-id scheme.}
\par {\f2\fs20 Apache::AuthCookie}{\fs20  uses the authentication and authorization phases of the request to provide an HTML login page.  Upon successful authentication, a session key is returned to the browser in the form of a cookie.  This session 
key is then used to authenticate future requests and can be used  to index into a database to retrieve user information such as the username.  The primary focus of }{\f2\fs20 Apache::AuthCookie}{\fs20  is user authentication and authorization.}{\up6
\fs20 \chftn }{\*\footnote \pard\plain \s4 \f1\cf1 {\up6\fs16 \chftn }{\fs16 As of when this was written, ownership of the }{\f2\fs16 Apache::AuthCookie}{\fs16  package was being transfered to new ownership.  The direction of future development is not 
entirely clear at this point and may render some comments in this paper obsolete.}}
\par {\f2\fs20 Apache::Session}{\fs20  provides the means to maintain server state across requests by issuing a session identifier to the browser as either a cookie or part of the URL.  The specifics are not part of the actual package and are left to 
the web site developer.  If the session id is set in the browser  with a cookie, then }{\f2\fs20 Apache::Session}{\fs20  and }{\f2\fs20 Apache::AuthCookie}{\fs20  may be combined to provide both session tracking and user authentication.}
\par {\fs20 As cookies are typically used for authentication, the secret identifying the user is only passed over the network once, with subsequent page requests using a token returned by the earlier authentication procedure.  This breaks the problem 
of measuring the risk into two problems.  First, the risk in passing the secret and associated information, and the second being the risk in passing the token.  If the token has a finite lifetime, this second risk can be much less than that with basic 
authentication, even though the various parameters are similar.  When giving a finite lifetime to the cookie used to identify a user once they have logged in, }{\i\fs20 T}{\dn6\fs16 1/2}{\fs20  should be much larger than the lifetime of the cookie.  
This value will be affected by how well the cookie can be used by another browser and possibly how is is encoded.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Ticket Authentication}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 The authentication schemes offered by HTTP 1.1 and the various cookie mechanisms publicly available do well when the secrets database is held on the same machine as the web site requiring authentication.  However, 
as soon as we are no longer able to maintain such a configuration, we must  consider other security risks besides the mere transmission of secrets from the browser and storage of secrets on the web site.}
\par {\fs20 If the network might be sniffed, the secrets must not pass over the network between the web site and the authentication database in the clear.  Any communication between the two systems must either be encrypted or involve a challenge}{
\fs12  }{\fs20 /}{\fs12  }{\fs20 response mechanism.  However, a challenge}{\fs12  }{\fs20 /}{\fs12  }{\fs20 response mechanism requires knowledge of the secret by the web site.}
\par {\fs20 The authentication server presents an authentication ticket to the browser when the browser has successfully authenticated itself to the server.  This ticket is handled as a cookie by the browser and may be used at any web site which is able 
to understand the ticket.}
\par {\fs20 In order for a client web site to understand who is presenting the ticket, the ticket must contain at least the identity of the user in a readily usable form such as a username or user id.  In addition, the ticket must contain an expiration 
time to limit the usefulness of the ticket if a third-party obtains the ticket.  In addition to these two requirements, any authentication ticket should contain at least the network portion of the browser's IP address if not the complete IP address, 
and a list of additional fields that may be used for authorization.  By including the IP address of the browser, anyone capturing a ticket must use it ``as is'' within a certain time period and must spoof the IP address of the browser.}
\par {\fs20 The information contained in the ticket is serialized and base-64 encoded to allow transmission as a cookie.  This encoded form is then hashed using the MD5 algorithm and the resulting hash (now a 128-bit number) signed.  Various signature 
algorithms are available, but due to patents and possible U. S. export restrictions, El Gamal is the algorithm of choice [10].  The two numbers comprising the El Gamal signature are prepended to the encoded, serialized ticket information and sent to 
the browser as a cookie.}
\par {\fs20 Once the browser has identified itself and obtained a ticket, the ticket may be used anywhere it is accepted until it expires.  On occasion, the browser may no longer need the ticket and will want to revoke it to disallow third-party use 
of the ticket.  This might happen when a user presses a ``logout'' button.  }
\par {\fs20 Because tickets are essentially stateless, once the ticket is issued, it cannot be taken back.  The digital signature verifies the authenticity of the ticket and the ticket contents identify the owner and duration of the ticket.  No other 
information is contained within the ticket.  }
\par {\fs20 To revoke a ticket, each entity which relies on the veracity of the tickets must know which tickets are invalid.  Each client web site must register with the ticket server and publish a well-known URL where the ticket server can PUT (referring 
to the HTTP method) known revoked tickets.  The ticket server must do this in such a way that an arbitrary client cannot revoke an arbitrary ticket.}
\par {\fs20 Ticket authentication carries much the same risks as other cookie based authentication mechanisms.  However, it is much more usual to include some type of IP address restrictions and lifetimes in the tickets than with session ids used elsewhere.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Authen::Ticket}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 The }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  package provides a toolkit which may be used to build the clients and server for a ticket based authentication scheme.  The toolkit not only includes ticket creation and 
verification, but also digital signatures of the tickets using the El Gamal algorithm.  The code is quite configurable and extensible.}
\par {\fs20 The }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  class itself provides the Apache server extensions to run the ticket server and handle authentication in the client web site.  This class cannot be used outside the mod_perl environment.  Every effort 
is made to isolate all mod_perl dependencies within the Authen::Ticket package and allow the }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::*}{\fs20  }{\fs20 classes to operate in any server environment from mod_perl to simple CGI.}
\par {\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  acts as a ticket server when it is installed as a content handler.  Using the }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Server}{\fs20  class, it will prompt the user for a username and password along with the duration for which 
the ticket will be valid and the amount of IP information to include in the ticket.  The default lifetime of a ticket is fifteen minutes.  The default IP information is the IP address of the browser.}
\par {\fs20 The }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Server}{\fs20  class provides all the code necessary for creating a ticket server.  This class is used by }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  to actually do the work in issuing tickets.  All the login and error 
screens, ticket construction and encoding, and user authentication code is contained within this class.}
\par {\fs20 The client class is designed to complement the ticket server and provides the code to verify an authentication ticket.  If the client class inherits from }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Signature}{\fs20  as well as }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Client}{
\fs20 , then the ticket must contain a valid signature.}
\par {\fs20 To use the client class, construct a new object with an optional reference to the Apache request structure.  If the request information is available (i.e., in a mod_perl environment), then the environmental variables normally used for passing 
information to CGI scripts will not be used.  Constructing the object will extract the ticket information and store it for later manipulation.  In addition, if the ticket signature code is available (e.g., in a derived class), then the signature is also 
verified at this time.}
\par {\fs20 To verify that the ticket is valid, use the }{\f2\fs20 verify}{\fs20  method without any arguments.  This will verify that the ticket has not expired and the ticket is being used by the appropriate browser.}
\par {\fs20 When designing a custom client module, the }{\f2\fs20 verify}{\fs20  method may be overridden to provide additional checks.  We recommend placing a call to the existing routine somewhere in the new one to ensure proper expiration and browser 
checks without having to rewrite that code.}
\par {\fs20 The }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Signature}{\fs20  class is an optional class which may be used to provide digital signatures.  In an environment with distributed and untrusted web sites, this class is a must.  This class not only provides 
the ticket signature creation and verification routines, but also provides key server and ticket revocation handlers.  The default signature algorithm is El Gamal with a nominally 512-bit key.  El Gamal is not encumbered with any patents [10].}
\par {\fs20 Digital signature generation and verification are mathematically intensive operations.  If the client web site and the ticket server are both trusted systems (e.g., operated by the same group), then signatures may be replaced with a simple 
hash of the encoded ticket information combined with a shared secret [11].}
\par \pard\plain \s6\qj\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Building a Ticket Scheme}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 To illustrate how }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  may be used to create a ticket scheme, let us assume a backend database stored in MySQL.  Our code will use the DBI/DBD modules to access the information.  
See Listings 1 and 2 for the MySQL and Perl code.  At a minimum, an authenticating function must be defined which can authenticate an individual based on a hash of values built from an HTML form.  If the default fields are insufficient, the form may 
be overridden with a customized page.}
\par {\fs20 The subclasses in Listing 2 do not provide for digital signatures.  To add a signature to the ticket, both }{\f2\fs20 @ISA}{\fs20  arrays (lines 11 and 40) must include a class comparable in function to }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Signature}{
\fs20 .  }
\par \pard\plain \fi720 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Given a proper Apache configuration, the code in Listing 2 is able to provide a login screen and confirmation of the secret, construct a properly signed ticket (with the additions mentioned from }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket::Signature}{
\fs20 ), provide the public key to the client web site for ticket verification, and verify tickets on the client web site.}
\par {\fs20 Ticket revocation in }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  }{\fs20 is currently under development.  Revocation places a fair burden on both client and server and should be designed with care.  Current design calls for client websites to register 
with the server, and the server to then notify the client sites of all revoked tickets.  Since the tickets have a finite lifetime, the clients only need track revoked tickets until they expire.}
\par \pard\plain \s6\keepn\fi1349\li-720\sb360\sa360 \b\f1\fs48\cf1 {\fs28 Conclusions}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 Ultimately, the methods and protocols used in an organization must be tailored to meet the needs and expectations of that organization.}{  }{\fs20 The security of any system depends not only on the services offered 
by a system and the quality of the code and/or protocols being used, but also on the environment.  This includes not only the physical location of the system, but also the network through which it communicates and the people interacting with it.  In 
addition, any collection of systems is only as secure as the least secure among them.}
\par \pard\plain \s8\phpg\absw4877\posx6278\pvmrg\posyt\nowrap\dxfrtext0 \f1\cf1 {\fs16 Listing 1. MySQL User Information Table Creation}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sa144\tqr\tx360\tx540\brdrt\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \phpg\absw4877\posx6278\pvmrg\posyt\nowrap\dxfrtext0 \f2\cf1 {\fs16 \tab 1\tab use test;\line
\tab 2\tab create table users (\line \tab 3\tab\tab username char(8),\line\tab 4\tab\tab password char(32)\line\tab 5\tab);}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 While the standard authentication methods provided in the HTTP standard are sufficient for those web sites with local control of both content and authentication information, they fall short of being a secure 
mechanism when the web site requiring the authentication is not trusted by the system providing the identity repository.  In those situations, a more secure method is required that not only considers the security of the secret between the browser and 
the web site, but also the security of the secret on all systems other than the central identity repository.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn\sb288\phpg\absw4780\posx6395\pvmrg\posyb\dxfrtext0 \f1\fs16\cf1 Listing 2. Perl Authentication Sub-class
\par \pard\plain \s1\sa144\tqr\tx360\tx540\brdrt\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrcf1\brdrw5 \phpg\absw4780\posx6395\pvmrg\posyb\dxfrtext0 \f2\cf1 {\fs16 \tab 1\tab package My::Auth;\line
\tab 2\tab use strict;\line\tab 3\tab use vars (qw/$VERSION @ISA/);\line\tab 4\tab$VERSION = '0.01';\line\tab 5\tab@ISA = (qw/Authen::Ticket/);}
\par {\fs16 \tab 6\tab package My::Auth::Server;\line\tab 7\tab use DBI;\line\tab 8\tab use DBD::mysql;\line\tab 9\tab use vars (qw/$VERSION @ISA %DEFAULTS/);\line\tab 10\tab$VERSION = '0.01';\line\tab 11\tab@ISA = (qw/Authen::Ticket::Server/);\line
\tab 12\tab%DEFAULTS = (\line\tab 13\tab    TicketUserDatabase => 'mysql:test',\line\tab 14\tab    TicketDatabaseUser => undef,\line\tab 15\tab    TicketDatabasePassword => undef,\line\tab 16\tab    TicketUserFields =>\line\tab 17\tab        'users:username:password',
\line\tab 18\tab);}
\par {\fs16 \tab 19\tab sub authenticate \{\line\tab 20\tab  my($self, $u) = @_;\line\tab 21\tab  my($table, $userfield, $passfield) =\line\tab 22\tab    split ':', $self->\{TicketUserFields\};\line\tab 23\line\tab 24\tab  tie my %DB, 'Tie::DBI', \{
\line\tab 25\tab    db => $self->\{TicketDatabase\},\line\tab 26\tab    table => $table, key => $userfield,\line\tab 27\tab  \} or return undef;\line\tab 28\line\tab 29\tab  return undef unless $DB\{$u->\{user\}\};\line\tab 30\line\tab 31\tab  my $sp 
= $DB\{$u->\{user\}\}->\{$passfield\};\line\tab 32\tab  return undef unless\line\tab 33\tab      $sp eq crypt($u->\{password\}, $sp);\line\tab 34\tab  return \{ \};\line\tab 35\tab\}}
\par {\fs16 \tab 36\tab package My::Auth::Client;\line\tab 37\tab use strict;\line\tab 38\tab use vars (qw/$VERSION @ISA/);\line\tab 39\tab$VERSION = '0.01';\line\tab 40\tab@ISA = (qw/Authen::Ticket::Client/);}
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi629 \f1\cf1 {\fs20 At present, no HTTP standard provides for a third-party authentication mechanism (e.g., kerberos) that does not require mass deployment of such objects as certificates (e.g., public key infrastructure).  Building 
a ticket scheme with }{\f2\fs20 Authen::Ticket}{\fs20  provides an intermediate system whereby temporary certificates are issued as required allowing time for such mass deployments or changes in the identity repository without inducing confusion among 
customers as system gradually convert. }
