\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 5   References}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 1. Introduction}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 HTTP provides for two authentication methods that MAY be used to determine the identity of the client.  In both methods, access to the effective password is REQUIRED for proper authentication.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 1.1. Goals}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The method described in this document is designed to meet the following criteria:}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Password information MUST not be stored on the authentication server in a format that may be of use to a browser.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 Authentication information MUST only be known by and presented to the authentication server.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The authentication client MUST be able to verify the validity of any issued authentication ticket.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The authentication ticket SHOULD NOT be easily used by an imposter.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 1.2. Reliance on the HTTP/1.1 Specification}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The method described in this document work within the HTTP/1.1 specification [1].  It uses the augmented BNF section 2.1 of that document, and relies on both the non-terminals defined in that document 
and other aspects of the HTTP/1.1 specification.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 1.3. Terminology}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119 [2].}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20       Authentication Server}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li1080\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The website from which browsers may obtain a proper authentication ticket.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20       Authentication Client}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li1080\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Any website which may make use of the authentication ticket issued by an Authentication Server for authentication purposes.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20       Authentication Ticket}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li1080\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 A cookie set on the browser by the Authentication Server for use by any Authentication Client.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20       browser}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li1080\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Any client as defined in the HTTP/1.1 specification [1].}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 1.4. Reference Implementation}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The reference implementation of the authentication method described in this document is the Authen::Ticket module available on CPAN.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 2. Authentication Ticket Construction}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Ticket is presented to the browser by the Authentication Server as a cookie with no expiration time.  The lifetime of the cookie is then the lifetime of the browser session.  The browser 
presents the Authentication Ticket to the Authentication Client as a cookie.  The browser SHOULD NOT need to understand the contents of the Authentication Ticket.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 2.1. Available Information}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li269\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Ticket MUST contain the following information:}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 uid      username}
\par {\f2\fs20 expiry   expiration time of ticket}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Ticket SHOULD contain the following information:}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 ip       IP address or network address of browser}
\par {\f2\fs20 fields   list of any optional fields included in ticket}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 2.2. Information Consolidation}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The informational portion of the Authentication Ticket is constructed by}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 ticket-info = 1#field}
\par {\f2\fs20 field       = token "=" (token | quoted_string)}
\par {\f2\fs20 token       = 1*<any CHAR except CTLs or separators>}
\par {\f2\fs20 separators  = "," | <">}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Example construction of `ticket-info':}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 uid => jgsmith}
\par {\f2\fs20 ip  => 165.91.250.23}
\par {\f2\fs20 expiry => 944119401}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 ticket-info = uid=jgsmith,ip=165.91.250.23,expiry=944119401}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 2.3. Information Encoding}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The information included in the Authentication Ticket is encoded to ensure proper handling by the client browser.  The actual method of encoding is not important but both the Authentication Client and 
Authentication Server MUST agree on the encoding method and the encoding method MUST be transparent to HTTP/1.1.  The encoding MUST be done before signing.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Note that the requirement for encoding the information before signing reduces the latency between receiving a ticket and verifying the signature by the Authentication Client.  This requirement in no way 
affects the security of the ticket.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The reference encoding is uuencoding (base 64).}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 2.4. Authentication Ticket Signature}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Server MUST sign the informational portion of the Authentication Ticket (which is the encoded ticket-info) and MUST include the resulting signature as a part of the Authentication Ticket.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 2.4.1. Signature Generation}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 A hash of the informational portion of the Authentication Ticket is signed by the Authentication Server.  The reference hash is MD5.  The reference signature algorithm is El Gamal.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 2.4.2. Signature Inclusion}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The RECOMMENDED format for including the signature within the Authentication Ticket is:}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 signed-ticket = signature ":" encoded-ticket-info}
\par {\f2\fs20 signature     = hex[ a ] ":" hex[ b ]}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Here, `a' and `b' represent the El Gamal signature of the encoded ticket-info.  The `hex' function produces a text hexadecimal form of its argument.  A similar signature format MAY be defined for any other 
signature algorithm.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The contents of signed-ticket MUST be transparent to HTTP/1.1 and understood by the Authentication Client.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 3. Key Server}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 Any Authentication Server MUST make public sufficient information to allow an arbitrary Authentication Client to verify any current ticket issued by the Authentication Server.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The format and method are implementation dependent.  The reference implementation provides keys in the following format for El Gamal.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li720\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 key = "y" ":" hex[}{\f2\fs12  }{\f2\fs20 y}{\f2\fs12  }{\f2\fs20 ] ";" "p" ":" hex[}{\f2\fs12  }{\f2\fs20 p}{\f2\fs12  }{\f2\fs20 ] ";" "g" ":" hex[}{\f2\fs12  }{\f2\fs20 g}{\f2\fs12  }{\f2\fs20 ]}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Client MUST NOT depend on any ordering of the key components.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The public key MAY be made available at a well known URL to satisfy the requirement to provide sufficient information to verify Authentication Tickets.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 4. Security}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 To ensure the security and authenticity of any tickets issued by an Authentication Server, certain actions must be taken by both the Authentication Server and the Authentication Client.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 4.1. Security Requirements of the Authentication Server}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Server MUST include sufficient information within the Authentication Ticket to identify the authenticated user to the satisfaction of a reasonable Authentication Client.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Server MUST sign the Authentication Ticket such that the signature verifies the authenticity of the issued Authentication Ticket.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Server SHOULD include the IP address of the browser requesting the Authentication Ticket.  The Authentication Server MUST include at least sufficient information in the Authentication Ticket to identify the network of 
the browser requesting the Authentication Ticket.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Server MUST include in the Authentication Ticket a finite expiration time.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 4.2. Security Requirements of the Authentication Client}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \li360\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Client MUST verify the signature of any Authentication Ticket before accepting the authenticity of the Authentication Ticket.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Client MUST reject any request from a browser whose IP address does not match the IP address contained within the Authentication Ticket.  If the Authentication Ticket only contains network information, then the browser 
must be on the indicated network.}
\par 
\par {\f2\fs20 The Authentication Client MUST reject any ticket which has expired.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par {\f2\fs20 5. References}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \fi-720\li1080\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 [1]\tab Fielding, R.,  Gettys, J., Mogul, J., Frysyk, H., Masinter, L., Leach, P. and T. Berners-Lee, "Hypertext Transfer Protocol -- HTTP/1.1", RFC 2616, June 1999.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 
\par \pard\plain \fi-720\li1080\ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 [2]\tab Bradner, S., "Key Words for Use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels", BCP 14, RFC 2119, March 1997.}
\par \pard\plain \ri1440 \f1\cf1 {\f2\fs20 \tab}
\par }
